# ─── Skynet environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env and fill in. Self-host: `docker compose up` reads this.

# Server
PORT=8080
NODE_ENV=development

# Persistence — REQUIRED (no silent default; the server refuses to boot if unset
# so ephemeral storage is never a surprise). The `pnpm dev` script sets memory.
#   STORE=memory    in-memory only, lost on restart (dev/tests)
#   STORE=file      zero-dependency JSON file at SKYNET_DB_PATH — durable, no DB
#                   or Docker needed (used by the desktop app)
#   STORE=postgres  durable multi-replica server deployments (compose provides it)
STORE=memory
SKYNET_DB_PATH=skynet-data.json
DATABASE_URL=postgres://skynet:skynet@localhost:5432/skynet
# A fresh deploy starts EMPTY — create your own projects + fleet runners in the UI
# (the first-run onboarding wizard walks you through it). There is no demo-seed flag.

# Real-time fan-out — REQUIRED (no silent default). BUS=memory is single-process
# (dev/tests); BUS=redis fans out across replicas (compose provides Redis).
BUS=memory
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# Orchestration — the execution backend is chosen PER AGENT from its fleet
# runner's provider (claude/codex/gemini/cursor/copilot/hermes), picked at agent
# creation. Provider keys come from the in-app secret store or env below.
# A real runner that can't launch (missing binary / auth) FAILS loudly
# (agent → needs-attention), instead of silently "completing" with no changes.
#
# There is no mock and no global override: a runner runs only if its provider
# has a credential (a key/token below or a stored secret) or is a CLI-login
# provider (cursor/copilot). No credential → nothing runs.
# SKYNET_RUNNER_CWD points a real runner at its working repo.
# SKYNET_RUNNER_CWD=/path/to/target/repo
#
# Opt-in OS sandbox for CLI-runner subprocesses (codex/gemini/cursor/copilot/
# hermes). When truthy AND a sandbox tool is present (macOS sandbox-exec, Linux
# bwrap), the runner's writes are confined to its worktree (+ temp and vendor
# cache/config dirs); reads and network stay open. Best-effort: off, unsupported
# platform, or missing tool → runs unsandboxed and logs why (never fails to
# start). Default off. A guardrail for a well-meaning agent, not a hard boundary.
# SKYNET_RUNNER_SANDBOX=true
#
# Runner resource cap — wall-clock ceiling (ms) on a single agent run before it's
# force-stopped (marked needs-attention). Bounds a runaway/hung agent so it can't
# hold its fleet slot and burn tokens forever. Applies to every runner (in-process
# Claude + CLI vendors). Default 1800000 (30 min); 0 disables. Memory/CPU/network
# caps require the containerized runner (see ROADMAP) — this is the cap that works
# without a container.
# SKYNET_RUNNER_MAX_RUNTIME_MS=1800000

# Integration & worktrees — the work→diff→merge loop. Unset SKYNET_INTEGRATION_REPO
# (default) keeps Phase 0 behavior: diff-approve just completes the agent, no git.
# When set, every agent gets its own git worktree on an `agent/*` branch cut from
# the project integration branch; on completion its diff is committed and reviewed,
# and approving enqueues the branch onto the serialized merge queue.
# SKYNET_INTEGRATION_REPO=/path/to/target/repo
# SKYNET_BASE_BRANCH=main
# SKYNET_CHECK_CMD=pnpm test            # optional: run in the repo before a merge commits
# SKYNET_WORKTREES_DIR=/path/to/worktrees  # default: <repo>/../.skynet-worktrees
# Where GitHub-connected projects are cloned on a server with no local checkout
# (headless/GCP): each gets <SKYNET_REPOS_DIR>/<projectId>, set as its repoPath.
# On the desktop you pick a local folder instead, so leave this unset there.
# SKYNET_REPOS_DIR=/data/repos
# The check command is safety-classified (a catastrophic command is refused) and
# run bounded: hard timeout, captured-output cap, and a scrubbed env allowlist
# (PATH/HOME/LANG/… only — not the full process env). These bounds also apply to
# any other command Skynet runs on your behalf; tune them if a legit check needs
# longer or emits a lot of output.
# SKYNET_CMD_TIMEOUT_MS=120000          # per-command wall-clock limit (default 120s)
# SKYNET_CMD_MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES=1000000   # per-stream capture cap before truncating (default 1 MiB)

# GitHub App — when set, an approved diff is pushed and a PR opened on GitHub
# (instead of the local merge queue) for projects bound to a repo, gated by the
# workspace's safety policy. Unset → the GitHub flow is off. See
# docs/github-integration.md. The private key is server-side only, never per-workspace.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=123456
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n…\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----"
# GITHUB_API_URL=https://api.github.com        # override for GitHub Enterprise
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=…                       # HMAC secret for inbound webhooks (§6)
#
# Inbound Sentry webhook (roadmap "Tools via MCP") — a new/regressed Sentry
# issue on a project's bound org/project (set in that project's settings)
# becomes a task. Unset → the webhook 404s (feature off). See
# docs/integrations-catalog.md and apps/server/src/sentry/webhook.ts.
# SENTRY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=…
#
# GitHub App via cloud token-broker (Phase 2 — the desktop path; the App private
# key stays on the broker, never on the device). Set BOTH to enable the real
# device-flow install in the UI. See services/github-broker/README.md.
#   SKYNET_GITHUB_BROKER_URL — the deployed gen-2 function URL (…/github-token-broker)
#   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID         — the App's public client id (Device Flow needs no secret)
# SKYNET_GITHUB_BROKER_URL=https://<region>-<project>.cloudfunctions.net/github-token-broker
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=Iv1.xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ── Telegram messaging bridge + remote kill switch (outbound-only) ───────────
# The installed desktop app connects OUT to Telegram (long-poll — NO hosting, NO
# open ports, NO inbound webhook). It pushes gate/run notifications to your phone
# and accepts a FEW owner-only slash-commands, including a remote kill switch.
# BYO bot: create one with @BotFather, paste its token below. Find your owner
# chat id by messaging the bot then checking getUpdates, or via @userinfobot.
#
# SECURITY: the bridge acts ONLY on messages from the owner chat id (all others
# ignored). The deterministic kill switch + status (/stop /quit /status /gates
# /help) are handled BEFORE any LLM parse and always work, with control OFF.
# Conversational control (SKYNET_TELEGRAM_CONTROL) is OPT-IN (default OFF). When
# ON, free text is interpreted by YOUR OWN provider key (BYOK) into a CLOSED
# five-action whitelist — approve · reject · add_task · assign · add_agent — and
# EVERY action is echoed back and executed ONLY after you reply "yes". The action
# space never exceeds those five, so a misparse or an injected instruction can't
# escalate. Commands: /status /gates /approve /reject /stop /resume /quit /help.
#   • /stop halts all live runs and pauses autonomy (processing stops).
#   • /quit shuts down the desktop app entirely.
# Unset either of the first two vars → the bridge stays disabled. NEVER commit the
# real token (or, on desktop, set these in <userData>/skynet.env instead).
#
# RECOMMENDED: set a SPEND / USAGE LIMIT on your provider API key in the vendor's
# console before enabling remote control or autonomy. The kill switch is reactive
# (you have to send /stop); a spend cap is the passive backstop that bounds the
# blast radius if something runs away while you're not watching.
# SKYNET_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456:ABC-DEF_your_botfather_token
# SKYNET_TELEGRAM_OWNER_CHAT_ID=123456789
# SKYNET_TELEGRAM_CONTROL=false   # set true for confirmed conversational control
# How long to wait before pinging Telegram about a diff/merge gate on an
# autonomy-on project. Long enough for the auto-reviewer (~15s autonomy tick +
# LLM call) to resolve auto-approvable gates before the phone buzzes. Real
# human-facing gates still come through with this delay. 0 disables (every
# gate pings immediately).
# SKYNET_TELEGRAM_GATE_DEBOUNCE_MS=20000

# Session backend — REQUIRED (no silent default). SESSIONS=memory (dev/tests),
# SESSIONS=postgres (durable), or SESSIONS=redis (multi-replica).
SESSIONS=memory

# Auth — when true, requests without a valid token are rejected (401). SECURE BY
# DEFAULT: it's ON unless NODE_ENV is EXPLICITLY development or test — so an
# unset/typo'd/"staging" NODE_ENV requires auth (fail closed). Dev tokens
# (dev-cyberdyne, dev-resistance) + the open default resolve ONLY in explicit
# dev/test. Setting AUTH_REQUIRED=false outside dev/test makes the server REFUSE
# TO BOOT rather than silently serve unauthenticated requests.
AUTH_REQUIRED=false

# Rate limiting (per-IP, per-minute) on /api + /mcp. General cap blunts abuse;
# login is far tighter to blunt password brute-force. Loopback is exempt in
# dev/test (trusted desktop). SKYNET_RATE_MAX=0 disables it.
SKYNET_RATE_MAX=600
SKYNET_LOGIN_RATE_MAX=10
# Behind a reverse proxy you control, trust X-Forwarded-For so the rate limiter
# keys on the real client IP (not the proxy). Leave false otherwise (XFF spoof).
SKYNET_TRUST_PROXY=false
# CORS cross-origin allowlist (comma-separated). In dev/test CORS is permissive
# (localhost dev is unaffected). In production-grade mode ONLY these origins are
# allowed; an EMPTY list defaults CLOSED (cross-origin rejected — no reflect-any
# fall-back). Same-origin / no-Origin requests always pass.
# SKYNET_CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.yourco.com,https://ops.yourco.com
# Real login: POST /api/auth/login {email,password} → session token + cookie.
# Dev seed credentials: jordan@cyberdyne.dev / kyle@resistance.dev (pw: skynet).
# The demo pair is seeded ONLY in dev/test — NEVER in production.
#
# First admin in PRODUCTION: the demo accounts aren't seeded, so seed the initial
# operator from these two vars (both required, or no operator is created and UI
# login is disabled — fine for a headless/MCP deploy that uses service tokens).
# SKYNET_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@yourco.com
# SKYNET_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-strong-secret
# Workspace the admin belongs to (default: the single-tenant default workspace).
# SKYNET_ADMIN_WORKSPACE=cyberdyne
# Session lifetime in ms before a token expires → 401 (default 12h).
# Two knobs — password-only sessions use the short one, MFA-verified sessions
# use the long one (default 30d). MFA already raised the security bar, so
# re-verifying every 12h is more friction than it buys.
SESSION_TTL_MS=43200000
SESSION_TTL_MFA_MS=2592000000

# Live preview (W5) — turns a visual agent's branch into a sandboxed,
# embeddable URL the SPA iframes. Off by default (agents fold the panel away).
#   PREVIEW=off       no previews (default)
#   PREVIEW=artifact  build the branch + serve it from this server's /preview
#   PREVIEW=deploy    format an external deploy/render URL from a template
PREVIEW=off
# Mark projects visual regardless of seed flag (comma-separated project ids).
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_VISUAL_PROJECTS=onboard,frontend
# Build pipeline (artifact mode). Branch source, in priority order:
#   a worktree of SKYNET_INTEGRATION_REPO → <SOURCE_ROOT>/<agentId> → SOURCE_DIR.
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_SOURCE_ROOT=/path/to/agent/worktrees
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_SOURCE_DIR=/path/to/a/buildable/site
# With no build command the source is published as static files (nothing runs).
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_INSTALL_CMD=npm ci
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_BUILD_CMD=npm run build
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_OUTPUT_DIR=dist
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_BUILD_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# Where built artifacts are written (default: <tmp>/skynet-previews).
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_ROOT=/var/lib/skynet/previews
# Serve previews from a separate origin in prod (sandbox); CSP frame-ancestors.
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_BASE_URL=https://preview.example.com
# SKYNET_PREVIEW_FRAME_ANCESTORS=https://app.example.com

# ── MCP (agents drive Skynet as a tool) — see docs/mcp.md ───────────────────
# Skynet exposes an MCP server at POST /mcp using the same bearer-token auth as
# /api. Tokens are minted from Settings → MCP access, or, for a HEADLESS/SANDBOX
# deploy (e.g. Daytona) where there is no human to log in, seeded from env below.
# The creating agent generates a STRONG random secret, injects it here, boots
# Skynet, then connects an MCP client to <url>/mcp with `Authorization: Bearer`.
# Unset (default) → no bootstrap token; run with AUTH_REQUIRED=true in a sandbox.
# SKYNET_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN=skynet_pat_replace_with_a_strong_random_secret
# Scopes for that token (comma list). Default observe,author. NEVER approver
# unless you intend the agent to resolve HITL gates without a human.
# SKYNET_BOOTSTRAP_SCOPES=observe,author
# Workspace the bootstrap token is scoped to (default: the single-tenant one).
# SKYNET_BOOTSTRAP_WORKSPACE=cyberdyne

# Provider API keys (only needed once real runners land — Phase 1+)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# cursor/copilot authenticate via their own CLI login (no key needed), but a
# token here also works: CURSOR_API_KEY / GITHUB_TOKEN.
#
# Hermes (Nous Research) — the `hermes` CLI is provider-agnostic; OpenRouter is
# its recommended key (a provider key like ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in the env also
# makes it usable). The runner drives `hermes -z "<task>"` headless, so the CLI
# must be installed and on PATH on the machine running the agent (see below).
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Override the hermes binary path, or pass extra argv (space-separated), if the
# CLI isn't on PATH or you need provider/model flags beyond the model selection.
# SKYNET_HERMES_BIN=hermes
# HERMES_EXTRA_ARGS=--provider openrouter

# Onboarding telemetry (PMF v1.5) — anonymous, opt-out install-event pings
# (workspace created / repo connected / key added / runner added / first task
# created), each firing at most once per workspace. Every workspace can also
# opt out individually in Settings (WorkspaceSettings.telemetryOptOut). Unset
# SKYNET_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT → milestones are still recorded locally (your own
# visibility) but nothing is ever sent over the network.
# SKYNET_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT=https://telemetry.example.com/ingest
# Hard global kill switch, independent of any workspace's own opt-out.
# SKYNET_TELEMETRY_DISABLE=true

# Web (Vite) — base URL the SPA uses to reach the API/WS.
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8080
